    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "1d4ecfbe",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Reflection Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why is row selection important?\n",
        "2. Why are unknown values often replaced with NaN?\n",
        "3. What is a skip pattern?\n",
        "4. Why should data management decisions be documented?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "7bd41409",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Part 5: Recoding Responses\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes response codes are technically correct but difficult to interpret. Analysts often recode values to make them more intuitive while preserving the meaning of the data. In this section you will reverse-code smoking frequency and create a quantitative approximation of smoking days per month.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "573e420a",
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "example"
        ]
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "recode1 = {\n",
        "    1:6,\n",
        "    2:5,\n",
        "    3:4,\n",
        "    4:3,\n",
        "    5:2,\n",
        "    6:1\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "nesarc_data['USFREQ'] = nesarc_data['S3AQ3B1'].map(recode1)\n",
        "\n",
        "nesarc_data['USFREQ'].value_counts(dropna=False)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "6ffd2e0c",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The new variable USFREQ is easier to interpret because higher values now correspond to more frequent smoking behaviour. This is often useful when creating visualisations and performing statistical analysis.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "b779f3dd",
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "example"
        ]
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "recode2 = {\n",
        "    1:30,\n",
        "    2:22,\n",
        "    3:14,\n",
        "    4:5,\n",
        "    5:2.5,\n",
        "    6:1\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "nesarc_data['USFREQMO'] = nesarc_data['S3AQ3B1'].map(recode2)\n",
        "\n",
        "nesarc_data['USFREQMO'].value_counts(dropna=False)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "271919c4",
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "exercise"
        ]
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Exercise\n",
        "# Compare the distributions for:\n",
        "# S3AQ3B1\n",
        "# USFREQ\n",
        "# USFREQMO\n",
        "# Which variable would be easiest to interpret and why?\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "7ced3e81",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Part 6: Renaming Variables and Simplifying the DataFrame\n",
        "\n",
        "Cryptic survey variable names make analysis difficult. Professional analysts almost always rename variables so reports and code are easier to understand.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "961eb429",
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "example"
        ]
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "nesarc_dict = {\n",
        " 'TAB12MDX':'NICOTINE_DEPENDENCE',\n",
        " 'CHECK321':'SMOKING_STATUS',\n",
        " 'S3AQ3B1':'FREQUENCY_OF_SMOKING',\n",
        " 'S3AQ3C1':'QUANTITY_SMOKED',\n",
        " 'S2AQ3':'DRANK_ALCOHOL',\n",
        " 'S2AQ8A':'ALCOHOL_FREQUENCY'\n",
        "}\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "9d341680",
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "example"
        ]
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "print('Renaming columns...')\n",
        "nesarc_data.rename(columns=nesarc_dict, inplace=True)\n",
        "print('Rename complete')\n"
      ]
    },
